# Assets Module

## Purpose

Data-driven asset loading pipeline. `ImageLoader` reads image files from disk; `TextureAtlas` maps subtexture names to UV rectangles from a JSON atlas file; `AnimationClip` defines a frame sequence (with per-frame durations) referencing a texture atlas.

## Static Sprites vs Animation

| Use case | Asset format | Loading path |
|---|---|---|
| **Static sprites** (ship, bullet, asteroid, background) | Standalone PNG per sprite | `RenderSystem::CreateTexture( path )` -> bindless index on `RenderComponent` |
| **Animated sprites** (in progress) | Atlas PNG + JSON + `.ftpp` clip | `AnimationClip` -> `AnimatedSprite` drives `RenderComponent::uv_rect` against one atlas texture |

`TextureAtlas` remains in use for the animation pipeline. Static game sprites no longer use atlas JSON for UV lookup -- each sprite is a standalone image registered in the bindless array.

## Key Classes

| Class | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `ImageLoader` | Wraps STB image; loads PNG/JPEG/BMP into a raw `Image` struct |
| `ImageLoader::Image` | POD: `data` (raw bytes), `width`, `height`, `num_channels` |
| `TextureAtlas` | Parses JSON atlas; maps subtexture name -> `SubTexture` (uv_min / uv_max); supports V-flip |
| `TextureAtlas::SubTexture` | `name`, `uv_min` (vec2), `uv_max` (vec2) |
| `AnimationClip` | Parses JSON clip definition; stores ordered `Frame` array |
| `AnimationClip::Frame` | `uv_min`, `uv_max`, `duration_sec` |

## API

```cpp
// Load a raw image (used internally by RenderSystem::CreateTexture)
auto img = ImageLoader::LoadImage( "textures/ship.png" );
// img.data, img.width, img.height, img.num_channels

// Static sprite (preferred for non-animated sprites -- from game/ECS layer):
uint32_t tex_index = render_system.CreateTexture( "resources/textures/ship/Ship.png" );

// Load a texture atlas (animation pipeline)
TextureAtlas atlas( "resources/textures/ship/ship_texatlas.json" );
auto sub = atlas.GetSubTexture( "ship_idle" );  // returns (0,0)-(1,1) default if not found
atlas.Flip();  // invert V for OpenGL-style coordinate systems

// Load an animation clip (references an atlas internally)
AnimationClip clip( "resources/textures/ship/ship.ftpp" );
int frame_count = clip.GetFrameCount();
auto frame = clip.GetFrame( 0 );  // frame.uv_min, frame.uv_max, frame.duration_sec
```

## JSON Formats

**TextureAtlas** (`*.json`):
```json
{
  "frames": [
    { "name": "ship_idle", "x": 0, "y": 0, "w": 64, "h": 64 }
  ],
  "meta": { "size": { "w": 256, "h": 256 } }
}
```
UVs are computed as `uv = pixel / atlas_size`.

**AnimationClip** (`*.ftpp`):
```json
{
  "atlas": "ship_texatlas.json",
  "flip": false,
  "frames": [
    { "name": "ship_idle_0", "duration": 0.1 },
    { "name": "ship_idle_1", "duration": 0.1 }
  ]
}
```

## Key Patterns

- **Composition:** `AnimationClip` loads a `TextureAtlas` internally and resolves frame UV rects at construction time -- no runtime atlas lookup during animation.
- **UV flip:** Different rendering backends use different Y-axis conventions. Call `atlas.Flip()` (or set `"flip": true` in clip JSON) when the atlas was authored top-origin.
- **Default fallback:** `GetSubTexture()` returns `(0,0)-(1,1)` if the name is not found -- logs a warning but won't crash.
- **STB ownership:** `Image::data` is heap-allocated by STB. Lifetime matches the `Image` struct.
- **Atlas slicing:** Standalone sprite PNGs can be exported from atlas JSON frame rects (see `tools/slice_atlas.py`).

## DOs

- Use `RenderSystem::CreateTexture()` for static sprite textures from standalone PNGs
- Use `AnimationClip` as the source of truth for animated sprites; don't hardcode UV rects
- Check `GetFrameCount() > 0` before using a clip (JSON parse failures produce empty clips)

## DON'Ts

- Don't use `TextureAtlas` for static game sprites -- use standalone PNGs + `CreateTexture()` instead
- Don't call `Flip()` on an atlas that's already been flipped -- UVs will be wrong
- Don't store `Image::data` pointer beyond the `Image` struct lifetime
- Don't bypass `AnimationClip` and manually index `TextureAtlas` for animation -- use `AnimatedSprite` from the animation module
